let's go to the other side of the hill  and bring in nebraska republican senator  deb fischer of the senate armed services  committee discuss today's hearing as  well as what we just heard from chad  senator thanks for joining us thank you  brett i want to play this sound bite  what the president told george  stephanopoulos and what you heard some  of it today about the troop  recommendations take a listen  your military advisor did not tell you  no we should just keep 2500 troops no no  one said that to me that i can recall is  that true i know the president to be an  honest and forthright man was that a  false statement to the americans i'm not  going to categorize the statement of a  president united states you do not have  a duty to cover for the president when  he's not telling the truth was that a  false statement or not i've given you my  opinion on the matter i've given you my  judgment on it  uh senator both of those generals the  chairman of the joint chiefs and general  mckenzie said that they recommended 2500  troops remaining on the ground in  afghanistan as they put it up the chain  right you know presidents are elected  for reasons they make strategic  decisions general milley said that today  he also said that this was a strategic  failure president biden disregarded the  advice of his top generals he knew what  the risks were he knew what the  consequences would be and he went ahead  and made his own decision on this you  know that's a it's really a pattern  we've seen with the president bob gates  made the observation in his book that  joe biden in 40 years in the united  states senate was wrong on just about  every serious foreign policy or national  security decision and he was wrong on  this one he should have listened to his  generals and he's been  not honest with the american people on a  number of things one was the advice that  he did receive he wasn't honest about  that you heard that today another was  his his position  that we can maintain  the security of this country by not  being in afghanistan and keeping a force  there with the with counterterrorism and  that was more my line of questioning  yeah what do you say when democrats push  back and say it's the president's  decision they call this a bold decision  even if it was in the face of his  military advisers at choosing something  different  because we had to get out of this  20-year war and we had to get our troops  home one way or another how do you  respond to that i respond by saying we  can disagree  on if we should have ever been in  afghanistan we can disagree  on the withdrawal in setting a date on  that but the american people have seen  the how wrong his decision is this isn't  a bold decision it's a wrong decision on  how we withdrew our troops i had a  nebraskan  corporal page who was killed in  afghanistan he was one of the 13. this  is a wrong decision by this president  and instead of trying to gloss it over  and and make excuses and try to spin it  at a press conference you know why can't  we look at a way forward and make right  decisions in the future senator wicker  asked the chairman about credibility  around the world take a listen to this  answer  our credibility would be damaged our  credibility has has  been gravely damaged has it not general  milley i think that our credibility um  with allies and partners around the  world and with adversaries  is being intensely reviewed by them to  see which way this is going to go  and i think that damage is one word that  could be used yes  damage could be used senator where do  you think this is going in the eyes of  our allies around the world well i was  on a codel over in eastern europe and i  talked to some nato allies and what we  heard was yes our credibility has been  damaged we're looking at allies who are  questioning  who we are if we are firm in our  commitments if we're going to be there  for them  in the future  and i can tell you one question that i'm  not hearing is from the jihadists from  the terrorists they're not questioning  this they're they're inspired they are  out recruiting and i think if you talk  to uh anyone within the department of  defense and with this in the state  department if they're honest they'll  tell you that recruitment's up for the  terrorists and it puts our nation at  risk  there are some democrats and others in  the finance committee who believe that  our credibility as a as a nation uh  paying their bills is going to be  affected and you voted no to raise the  debt ceiling  this week  as did other republicans  you voted that way before we should  point out under the trump administration  but are you worried that we're pushing  the line here as far as the financial  credibility  we aren't pushing the line because the  democrats can do this on their own they  you know they can do it on their own  they are right now talking about human  infrastructure and the  a 3.5 trillion dollar wish list for  progressives you know if they can do  that on their own they can pay the bills  on their own for it  but  isn't this a bipartisan thing it's a  bill that we've already paid i mean  we've already used that money this is  money that republicans  authorized as well uh  under the trump administration some 7.8  trillion dollars of debt was added to  the bottom line you know if you look at  what happened in march brett  that was a complete democrat exercise  that 1.9 trillion dollars we're seeing  it again right now they are not reaching  out in a bipartisan way they are not  reaching out to republicans even trying  to work on some ideas here i heard you  say earlier about the democrats want to  have a transformational change here in  our country  they want to do that they should do it  on their own then and they should pay  the bills they should be honest about  the cost that we're going to incur that  taxpayers are going to face and this  idea  that it's free or this idea that nobody  who makes under 400 000 is going to pay  taxes look at what the stock market did  today people understand what's going on  here nothing's free  everybody's going to be taxed you look  at inflation going up that's a tax on  the middle class and it is a tax on  working americans but senator you can  see that both parties republicans and  democrats have been spending like  drunken sailors for years up on capitol  hill  you know i've said that before too and  then we get hit with coven yeah and and  we work together in a bipartisan way  trying to address that and we did spend  six trillion dollars but i think  republicans are saying you know we  didn't need to do that 1.9 trillion in  march because let's go back and see  where that other money has been spent on  and look at what's still available  what programs work that's the difference  you're seeing between the two parties  last thing now last thing quickly  senator senator bernie sanders  has said to house members on the  progressive side that if they vote on  this bipartisan infrastructure bill  thursday uh that they are losing all  leverage and he's saying don't vote on  that first you need to get the big 3.5  trillion dollar plan where do you sense  this is right now as we kind of go  minute to minute up there on the hill  yeah you know it is minute to minute we  we don't know where the democrats are on  this they're going to have to figure  that out themselves but to change horses  in midstream is maybe it's not unusual  in washington but when you're told one  thing and then you see leadership with  nancy pelosi in the house trying to  maneuver that fine line between her  moderates and the progressive wing of  the party you know we're just well i'm  just going to sit back and watch  senator deb fischer we appreciate your  time thanks

